{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nS1 proves one atomic update executor and S2 produces pure plans, but legacy batch dispatch still replays compound methods, conflates deferred and applied work, and has no endpoint-failure fuse and relies on ad-hoc nested per-call retries that cannot distinguish physical attempts from exhausted logical outcomes (the only breaker today is the blast-radius mass-mutation guard `classify.check_blast_radius`, which is a different concern). One ticket-local or permanent failure can distort batch behavior.\n\n## Outcome\nMake the typed coordinator authoritative for non-create plans: update, delete, probe, clean-label, repair-property, and conflict handling. Execute atomic desired postconditions in dependency order; after partial success, re-observe and plan only remaining drift. Produce exact ticket/pass outcomes and apply the approved endpoint/provider finite-pass fuse only to normalized exhausted logical failures.\n\n## Plan\n1. Add a coordinator that consumes immutable plans, enforces dependencies, and calls adapter atomic operations through S1s budget. Preserve successful earlier postconditions and never replay the whole compound mutation after a late failure.\n2. Normalize auto-healed, success, deferred, permanent, commit-unknown, exhausted transient, and skipped dispositions with ticket/endpoint/tenant/provider/global scope.\n3. Add a new endpoint-failure scoped fuse (none exists today). Endpoint scope opens after three consecutive eligible exhausted logical outcomes across at least two tickets; provider scope additionally spans two endpoint families. A matching success resets consecutive state. The existing blast-radius mass-mutation guard (`classify.check_blast_radius`) is a distinct protection and is retained unchanged.\n4. Exclude physical attempts, recovered warnings, permanent failures, ticket-local 404, and opaque diagnostics from inferred fuse counters. Retain direct authoritative credential, configuration, quota, rate, issue, endpoint, tenant, and global behavior.\n5. When a fuse opens, defer remaining matching plans with reason/scope/retry metadata while independent scopes continue. Preserve exact applied/failed/deferred/skipped tallies and nonzero degraded-pass signaling.\n\n## Testing (RED → GREEN)\n- Coordinator tables cover dependency success/failure, partial compound update, idempotent already-gone delete, ticket-local 404 isolation, permanent 4xx, auth/quota/rate scopes, and mixed endpoint families.\n- Fuse sequences assert threshold, two-ticket/two-family requirements, success reset, exclusions, and independent-scope continuation.\n- Replay fixtures derived from historical GitHub Actions outcomes assert zero premature stop in the completed 1,200-run slice; raw warning/grep text is never classified as a terminal outcome.\n- Verified-fake Cloud/DC batches assert wire order, maximum calls, exact tallies, logs, and exit status.\n\n## Compatibility / rollback\nCut over by non-create operation family and never dual-send. Before legacy removal, an operation-family flag can select the old dispatcher. Rollback preserves successful Jira effects and resumes from re-observation. The intentional differences are three-call shared budgeting, explicit deferral, exact partial outcomes, and a new endpoint-failure scoped fuse (the blast-radius mass-mutation guard is retained).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Non-create plans execute atomic postconditions once in dependency order; a late failure preserves earlier success and re-observes only remaining drift within the same logical budget.\n- [ ] Ticket-local/permanent failures isolate the ticket, authoritative broad scopes stop only their declared scope, and independent tickets/endpoints continue.\n- [ ] Endpoint fuse opens only after three consecutive eligible exhausted logical outcomes across two tickets; provider fuse additionally requires two endpoint families; matching success resets state.\n- [ ] Physical attempts, recovered warnings, permanent failures, ticket-local 404s, and opaque diagnostics never increment inferred fuse counters.\n- [ ] Remaining matching work is explicitly deferred with reason/scope/`retry_not_before`; applied, failed, deferred, skipped, and recovered tallies plus exit status are exact.\n- [ ] Historical replay fixtures and verified-fake Cloud/DC batches show no premature termination of observed successful/partially successful work.\n## Fuse counter semantics (resolves G6: durability + ordering basis)\nThe scoped finite-pass fuse's \"three consecutive eligible exhausted logical outcomes\" counter is:\n- **Per-pass and in-memory, never durable across passes.** The coordinator holds the counter only for the duration of one `reconcile.py::reconcile_once(pass_id)` pass and starts every scope at zero each pass. This is deliberate and consistent with S2: each pass re-observes into a fresh immutable `Observation`/`observation_version`, so the coordinator carries **no durable state between passes**. An endpoint that keeps failing simply re-trips the fuse in each subsequent pass once that pass alone accumulates the threshold; cross-pass persistence is neither required nor relied upon.\n- **Counted per scope, not globally.** Each fuse scope (endpoint family; provider = ≥2 endpoint families) owns its own consecutive counter. \"Consecutive\" is evaluated over the coordinator's **deterministic dependency-ordered execution sequence within the pass**, counting only the eligible exhausted-transient (`exhausted_transient`) outcomes **attributed to that scope**. Outcomes belonging to other scopes are interleaved freely and neither increment nor reset a given scope's counter — this is exactly what lets \"independent scopes continue\" while one scope trips.\n- **Reset rule.** A matching **success** (`applied`/`recovered`/`already_satisfied`) attributed to the same scope resets that scope's counter to zero. Only the exclusions in step 4 (physical attempts, recovered warnings, permanent failures, ticket-local 404, opaque diagnostics) are never counted at all.\n- **Threshold semantics.** Endpoint scope opens after 3 consecutive eligible exhausted outcomes spanning ≥2 distinct tickets in that scope within the pass; provider scope additionally requires the failing outcomes to span ≥2 endpoint families. Once open, remaining matching plans in that pass are deferred with reason/scope/`retry_not_before`; independent scopes keep executing.\n\n(AC line 3 is read under these semantics: \"consecutive\" = per-scope, per-pass, over dependency-ordered execution; \"resets\" = a same-scope success within the same pass.)"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787626723514887001,"uuid":"ad9cbff7-0a98-4564-887b-85948fce39fb"}